# Citation metadata.
#
# GitHub reads this file and puts a "Cite this repository" button on the
# repository page; Zenodo reads it when minting a DOI for a release. Both
# matter for a language rather than being paperwork: a language that cannot be
# cited cannot be referenced by the papers, encyclopedia entries and language
# surveys that decide whether it is recognised as one.
#
# `doi:` under `identifiers` is deliberately absent until the first Zenodo
# release mints one. Do not invent it.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use eTamil, please cite it as below."
title: "eTamil: a Tamil-vocabulary programming language for Indian accounting, tax and FinTech"
abbreviation: eTamil
type: software
version: 1.4.0
license: AGPL-3.0-or-later
repository-code: "https://github.com/Maruff/etamil_compiler"
url: "https://etamil.in"
keywords:
  - programming language
  - domain-specific language
  - Tamil
  - Tamil computing
  - fintech
  - accounting
  - taxation
  - GST
  - India
  - Rust
  - LLVM
  - decimal arithmetic
authors:
  - family-names: Maruff
    given-names: Mohammed
    alias: "Esan Maruff"
    email: esan@etamil.in
    affiliation: >-
      Department of Computer Applications, School of Computer, Information and
      Mathematical Sciences, B. S. Abdur Rahman Crescent Institute of Science
      and Technology, Chennai, India
abstract: >-
  eTamil is a programming language whose keywords are Tamil words, designed for
  Indian accounting, taxation and financial technology. Finance is part of the
  vocabulary rather than a library over it: credit, debit, tax, ledger and
  balance sheet are language constructs. Every keyword accepts three
  interchangeable spellings — Tamil script, a romanized transliteration typable
  on an unmodified keyboard, and for some an English alias — so a program can be
  written and read in either script. Every number is a fixed-point decimal
  rather than a binary float, because the language is intended for computations
  that must balance to the paisa. The implementation is written in Rust and
  comprises a lexer, parser, type checker, bytecode virtual machine and an
  optional LLVM backend, with a standard library and a double-entry accounting
  framework written in eTamil itself.

# The design paper. Update `year`, add `doi:` and replace `type:` with the
# published form once it appears in a venue — not before.
